doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX
abstract: >-
  Analysis code and de-identified data from a semi-structured interview
  study of 46 whole-building life cycle assessment (wbLCA) practitioners
  practising primarily in the United States, with a small number based in
  Canada. Participants comprise 20 architects,
  20 engineers, and 6 sustainability consultants, recruited from the
  SE 2050 and AIA 2030 commitment programs and from firms belonging to
  neither. The study examines practitioner demographics, LCA tool usage,
  time allocation, team structure, material quantity takeoff workflows,
  embodied carbon data sources, problems encountered in practice, and
  receptiveness to parametric and dynamic LCA methods. Interview
  responses were coded inductively; all reported counts are counts of
  participants who volunteered a topic and should be read as lower
  bounds on prevalence rather than as response rates.
keywords:
  - life cycle assessment
  - whole-building LCA
  - embodied carbon
  - building information modeling
  - qualitative research
  - practitioner interviews
  - architecture engineering construction
